feat(observability): land M4 telemetry stack (full metric coverage + OTLP traces + build_info)

* Stream-counted bytes: new CountingBody wraps the inbound (request) and
  outbound (response) http_body streams so corx_bytes_transferred_total
  {direction} actually moves on every chunk that flows through the proxy.
  pin-project-lite preserves Unsync, matching hyper's client body
  requirements.
* Full metric description set: REDIRECT_HOPS / SSRF_BLOCKS / DNS_LOOKUPS /
  WEBSOCKET_ACTIVE / WEBSOCKET_HANDSHAKES / CONFIG_RELOAD / BUILD_INFO are
  all describe_*-registered up front so /metrics carries HELP and TYPE
  metadata even before the first event fires. Latency histograms share a
  1ms..10s bucket layout; redirect-hops use a Fibonacci-flavoured 0..13
  layout aligned with the upstream redirect cap.
* DNS_LOOKUPS instrumentation: corx-core::proxy::ssrf::resolve increments
  the counter under result=literal | ok | error so operators can spot
  resolver outages and rebinding-style spikes from a single panel.
* build_info: a one-shot gauge stamped with version, rust_version and the
  active feature set (tls / mtls / fips / otel) lets dashboards confirm
  which binary is running without exec'ing into the pod.
* OpenTelemetry / OTLP (feature otel): new corx-server::observability::
  otel module builds an opentelemetry_sdk Tracer (gRPC or HTTP wire
  protocol, configurable sampler + service.name/namespace/version
  resource attributes) and the tracing-opentelemetry layer is spliced
  into the same registry as the structured-log subscriber so log records
  and trace spans share a single context.
* OtelConfig schema: new observability.otel sub-table with enabled,
  endpoint, protocol, service_name, service_namespace, resource_attributes,
  sample_ratio. Defaults are inert (disabled) so nothing changes for
  operators who do not opt in.

68 unit tests passing across the workspace with --all-features.
